Roast.pet Weird Builder Projects =E2=80=94 5 useful projects

This run screened 25+ plausible projects across GitHub, Hugging Face,
WebAR, browser video/caption tooling, pet CV and Etsy/POD automation,
then removed projects already surfaced in prior runs. Five are worth
retaining.

-

tscaps =E2=80=94 https://github.com/francozanardi/tscaps

Creator: Franco Zanardi. Current project; 18 stars / 4 forks in
surfaced metadata. License: engine MIT, templates MIT, studio
AGPL-3.0. Solves: browser-local caption composition and final video
export without a server. Install: npm install @tscaps/engine.
Dependencies: modern browser WebCodecs/Web Audio/Canvas; Node only for
development. Evidence: working local editor; captions are ordinary
HTML/CSS rasterized per frame; accepts existing subtitle data or
hand-built Documents, so Roast.pet can skip Whisper entirely and feed
known TTS timings. Work replaced: caption renderer, CSS styling
system, video compositing/export. Integration: LOW-MEDIUM. Maintenance
risk: MEDIUM because API is pre-1.0; pin a version. Experiment: feed
Kokoro word timestamps directly into a Document, render the canonical
15-second roast locally, compare output/time/LOC with ASS+FFmpeg.
Verdict: REUSE NOW / BENCHMARK.

-

rlogank/etsy-api-toolkit =E2=80=94 https://github.com/rlogank/etsy-api-tool=
kit

Creator: rlogank. Surfaced last week; tiny zero-star toolkit. License
file present; verify exact terms before copying. Solves: the boring
Etsy v3 plumbing=E2=80=94PKCE OAuth, refresh, snapshots, JSON plans, multip=
art
image/file uploads, draft listing creation. Install: plain Python
scripts; standard library only. Evidence: publish_to_etsy.py consumes
manifest.json; dry-run apply path; explicit token/customer-data
hygiene. Work replaced: most bespoke Etsy API wrapper and
listing-upload orchestration. Integration: LOW. Maintenance risk:
MEDIUM because Etsy APIs can change and repo is tiny. Experiment:
create a sandbox/test draft from one Roast.pet order manifest with
listing images and files; confirm upload order and refresh-token
behavior. Verdict: REUSE/COPY MECHANISM after license check.

-

chriz-3656/echoframe-mindar =E2=80=94 https://github.com/chriz-3656/echofra=
me-mindar

Creator: Albasith / chriz-3656. 4 commits, 1 star. License: MIT.
Solves: almost comically exact Roast.pet physical-card AR primitive:
recognize a printed image target and play a video directly on top of
it in a mobile browser. Install: static HTML/JS + MindAR +
targets.mind + MP4; serve over HTTPS. Dependencies/hardware: phone
browser/camera only. Evidence: complete repo is essentially
index.html, targets.mind and memory.mp4. Work replaced: initial
card=E2=86=92camera=E2=86=92video-overlay AR prototype. Integration: LOW. M=
aintenance
risk: LOW-MEDIUM, because MindAR is the real dependency. Experiment:
replace target with the canonical Roast.pet card and memory.mp4 with
one roast; test iPhone/Android acquisition time, jitter and
loss/reacquisition. Verdict: REUSE NOW for AR checkpoint.

-

Everidea-Interactive/hmns-webAR =E2=80=94
https://github.com/Everidea-Interactive/hmns-webAR

Creator: Everidea Interactive. Surfaced last week. License: MIT.
Solves: a more production-ish MindAR shell with mobile responsive UI,
image target tracking, screenshot and recording, status feedback and
GLTF animation support. Install: static web project; HTTPS required.
Dependencies: MindAR.js + Three.js. Evidence: screenshot/recording
functions and mobile browser compatibility are documented; target and
model paths are cleanly separated. Work replaced: AR sharing/recording
controls and mobile UX boilerplate. Integration: LOW. Maintenance
risk: MEDIUM. Experiment: swap its GLTF scene for our video/2D pet
layer and see whether its recording/share path can capture the
Roast.pet AR reaction for social sharing. Verdict: COPY SHELL / WATCH.

-

tejaswigowda/ffmpeg-webCLI =E2=80=94 https://github.com/tejaswigowda/ffmpeg=
-webCLI

Creator: Tejaswi Gowda / Arizona State University. 1.4k stars, 131
forks, 138 commits; surfaced today. License: GPL-3.0; ffmpeg.wasm
underlying LGPL-2.1. Solves: local browser post-processing=E2=80=94format
conversion, compression, trim, crop, overlay, audio mix, GIF, metadata
stripping, captions and local Whisper=E2=80=94with zero server upload.
Install: static PWA plus COOP/COEP headers for SharedArrayBuffer.
Evidence: live app, benchmarks/demos, and an August 2026 Web-CLI
architecture paper; runs processing in browser/Web Workers. Work
replaced: a large set of boring final-media utilities and
privacy-sensitive customer-video handling. Integration: MEDIUM because
GPL boundary matters and ffmpeg.wasm is heavier than WebCodecs.
Maintenance risk: LOW-MEDIUM. Experiment: use only its mechanisms as a
post-export worker: take a Roast.pet MP4, compress to social target,
strip metadata, make GIF preview and hard-burn captions; benchmark on
phone and weak laptop. Verdict: COPY MECHANISMS / WATCH; don=E2=80=99t embe=
d
blindly due GPL.

TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES

- Browser caption rendering/export: benchmark the MIT tscaps engine first.

- Etsy OAuth/listing/upload plumbing: adapt the tiny etsy-api-toolkit
rather than wrapping Etsy from scratch.

- Card=E2=86=92AR-video proof of concept: EchoFrame is already the exact
four-file MindAR demo.

- AR screenshot/record/share UI: borrow the hmns-webAR shell.

- Generic media post-processing: use established FFmpeg/WebCodecs
mechanisms rather than writing compressors/croppers/caption burners.

Most surprising hobby project: EchoFrame. A one-star, four-commit repo
already expresses the core Roast.pet physical product promise=E2=80=94point=
 a
phone at a printed card and have its associated video appear on the
card=E2=80=94in almost the smallest possible codebase.

Fastest integration win: EchoFrame. Replace targets.mind and
memory.mp4 and deploy one static HTTPS page. This should validate the
entire card=E2=86=92AR experience before any custom AR engineering.

Repo/author worth following: Franco Zanardi / tscaps. Its MIT engine
treats captions as HTML/CSS and renders entirely in-browser through
WebCodecs, which fits Roast.pet=E2=80=99s deterministic HTML-first media
architecture unusually well.

Architectural delta this run: the AR and commerce edges can both be
extremely thin. The likely V1 path is approved pet package +
deterministic HTML/video renderer + tscaps/ASS captions + static
MindAR target page + manifest-driven Etsy publisher.
Roast.pet-specific engineering should stay concentrated in pet
identity, comedy/performance and QA=E2=80=94not caption editors, AR shells,
Etsy OAuth or generic media utilities.
